1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Ops starts the tolling roadside service
with: Look at this bash for a moment. The
process still opens express-lane pricing.
A ConfigMap file says true. An environment
variable says true. The CLI says false.

2
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:27,000
Nobody can explain precedence without
opening Spring's property source order
charts. Feature flags and DB URLs for
tolling are supposed to resolve from env,
files, and CLI through one abstraction:
Environment.

3
00:00:27,119 --> 00:00:36,199
When that abstraction is ignored — or when
code reads System.getenv in one place and
@Value in another — operators lose the
kill switch.

4
00:00:36,319 --> 00:00:42,079
Environment models two ideas together:
active profiles, and a hierarchy of
PropertySource instances.

5
00:00:42,199 --> 00:00:52,239
When you call
env.getProperty("tolling.express-
lane.enabled"), Spring walks property
sources in precedence order and returns
the first hit.

6
00:00:52,359 --> 00:01:04,079
Boot adds several sources for you —
command-line args typically beat
environment variables, which beat
application YAML inside the jar, and so on
— but the interface you code against stays
Environment,

7
00:01:04,199 --> 00:01:07,719
or @Value / @ConfigurationProperties that
read through it.

8
00:01:07,839 --> 00:01:17,159
Profiles decide which documents join the
stack; Environment is what answers after
the stack is built. Look at this java for
a moment. Look at this example.

9
00:01:17,279 --> 00:01:31,359
# application.yml (lowest among these
examples) Walk the service. The
constructor captures resolved values once
at bean creation — a snapshot suitable for
flags that should not flip mid-request
without a refresh story.

10
00:01:31,479 --> 00:01:39,919
getProperty(key, Boolean.class, false)
converts the string through Spring's
conversion service and defaults to false
if the key is absent everywhere.

11
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:47,439
getRequiredProperty throws
IllegalStateException if the datasource
URL is missing — fail startup rather than
connect to a phantom default.

12
00:01:47,559 --> 00:01:59,759
price uses the captured boolean; it never
re-queries Environment per vehicle, so a
later change to a mounted file would not
be visible until the bean is rebuilt.
Runtime for that CLI launch.

13
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:12,559
Boot (or a manually prepared context)
builds the Environment *before* refreshing
most application beans. Command-line
property source is consulted first for
tolling.express-lane.enabled → false.

14
00:02:12,679 --> 00:02:23,559
The YAML true and any ConfigMap-derived
env var never win for that key.
LanePricingService constructs with false;
express discounts stay off.

15
00:02:23,679 --> 00:02:30,799
Change only the ConfigMap, omit the CLI
flag, restart, and the same code path
yields true without recompilation.

16
00:02:30,919 --> 00:02:47,239
If someone adds @Value("${tolling.express-
lane.enabled}") on another bean while a
third class calls Boolean.parseBoolean(Sys
tem.getenv("TOLLING_EXPRESS_LANE_ENABLED")
),

17
00:02:47,359 --> 00:02:52,879
you can get split decisions inside one
process — the failure mode that made ops
distrust the kill switch.

18
00:02:52,999 --> 00:02:57,719
Failure mode symptoms: CLI says false,
plaza receipts still show express
discounts.

19
00:02:57,839 --> 00:03:11,239
Checklist: confirm the property key
spelling matches exactly (relaxed binding
helps for env vars like
TOLLING_EXPRESS_LANE_ENABLED, but only
when read *through* Environment);

20
00:03:11,359 --> 00:03:23,679
confirm the bean actually injected
Environment/@Value rather than a static
getenv; dump high-precedence sources via a
secured actuator env endpoint and look for
an unexpected winner;

21
00:03:23,799 --> 00:03:30,239
confirm the flag was not read only into a
@Bean that was cached before you thought
you changed config.

22
00:03:30,359 --> 00:03:43,719
Another symptom: getRequiredProperty fails
at startup naming spring.datasource.url
even though DATABASE_URL is set — wrong
key, or a custom property source never
added. Trade-offs.

23
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:50,079
Reading Environment in constructors is
simple and testable with a
MockEnvironment; it freezes values at
creation.

24
00:03:50,199 --> 00:03:58,919
Re-querying Environment per call picks up
refreshed property sources if you adopt a
refresh mechanism, at the cost of
scattered lookups and harder reasoning.

25
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:07,719
@ConfigurationProperties groups keys with
validation — usually better than many raw
getProperty calls — but still sits on the
same precedence stack.

26
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:17,759
Bypassing Environment with System.getenv
or System.getProperty is occasionally
needed for code outside Spring; inside
beans it is how precedence bugs are born.

27
00:04:17,879 --> 00:04:32,879
Misconception unique to Environment:
"System.getenv and Environment.getProperty
always see the same map." Environment
includes env vars as one source, but also
JVM system properties, config files,
random value sources,

28
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,480
and command-line args, with precedence.

29
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:44,439
Reading System.getenv bypasses that merge
and can disagree with what Spring injected
elsewhere. Tolling pricing finally
respects the CLI kill switch.

30
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,759
The remaining mystery is sharper: which
exact property sources sit in the chain,
who adds them,

31
00:04:49,879 --> 00:05:00,480
and how Boot's opinionated startup
sequence wires that chain before your
first bean constructor runs — the reason
teams reach for Spring Boot instead of
assembling contexts by hand.
